# lexical-locals-escape-analysis — binding a local lexically when it provably cannot escape

STATUS: DONE (slice 1, clautolisp 2.2.143, 2026-09-28) — see Resolution. This file was
the analysis he asked for: can we know, at compile time, every function
we might call at run time?

## The optimization

At (SPEED 3) (DEBUG 0), a local that is not free in its scope and that
provably does not escape can be bound LEXICALLY -- a stack slot or a
register -- instead of a dynamic binding in a frame. The symbol's value
is then never touched.

The prize is large and already measured from the other side. In the
current profile of compiled code the usubr call machinery is the biggest
cluster left: BIND-USUBR-FRAME 34% cumulative, MAKE-DYNAMIC-BINDING 9.6%
self, MAKE-DYNAMIC-FRAME 3%, plus (SETF FRAME-BINDING). A lexical local
costs none of it -- no frame, no binding, no chain walk on every read.

pjb: BricsCAD does this, is NOT careful about the check, and so the
optimization is DETECTABLE by the change in semantics it implies -- at
which point one has to turn it off.

## What escape means here, and why it is hard

There are no closures, so a local does not escape by being captured. It
escapes because AutoLISP IS DYNAMICALLY SCOPED: anything the scope calls,
transitively, can read or write the name simply by using it free.

So the question pjb poses is exactly the right one -- do we know the
callees at compile time? -- and the honest answer is NO, not in general:

  - EVAL and READ build code at run time.
  - APPLY / MAPCAR with a function VALUE computed at run time.
  - LOAD brings in code after this function was compiled.
  - A later DEFUN, or a SETQ of a lambda, REDEFINES a callee.
  - COMMAND re-enters LISP.
  - *error* runs WITH THE FRAME CHAIN LIVE and can read any name. This is
    the one that would otherwise sink the whole idea: any form can
    signal, so every scope is reachable by a handler that names the
    local.
  - In clautolisp specifically, the debugger reads and WRITES bindings at
    a stop (spec 9.4, 9.5). pjb already anticipated this by tying the
    optimization to DEBUG 0, which is exactly right: at DEBUG 0 there is
    no instrumented fork, so there is nowhere to stop.

## A design that is sound rather than merely careful

Three parts, and each reuses machinery this engine already has.

1. CLOSED WORLD BY CONSTRUCTION. At compile time compute the set of
   functions transitively reachable from the scope, using the definitions
   in force. Refuse to optimize unless every one is statically known and
   none of them -- nor the scope itself -- calls a builtin that can
   invoke arbitrary AutoLISP: EVAL, APPLY, MAPCAR, COMMAND, LOAD,
   VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY, the VLAX callbacks. This is conservative and it is
   checkable. Note it excludes EVAL transitively: a callee that calls
   EVAL puts EVAL in the reachable set.

2. A GENERATION GUARD for the open world. The closed world is only true
   at the instant it was computed; a later DEFUN or LOAD can add a
   reference. So stamp the compiled body with a DEFINITION GENERATION
   counter, bumped by every defun / setq-of-a-lambda / load, and check it
   on entry: if the world moved, run the dynamically-bound body instead.
   Both pieces exist -- the generation-counter pattern from slice 6
   (*SPECIAL-OPERATOR-GENERATION*), and the two-bodies dispatch that
   already chooses between forks per call.

3. MATERIALISE ON THE ERROR PATH. The *error* objection is answered by
   paying only when it happens: the compiled function's unwind path
   pushes a real dynamic frame holding the lexical variables' current
   values before the handler runs. An error is rare; a handler that reads
   a caller's local then still sees it. This is what turns "unlikely to
   be noticed" into "cannot be noticed", and it is the difference between
   this and what pjb describes BricsCAD doing.

## Residual risks to state plainly

  - A reactor or CAD event firing into LISP from inside the scope is not
    in the reachable set. Rare, and arguably already outside what any
    engine promises here, but it is a hole.
  - Part 1 is conservative enough that the first version will optimize
    only leaf-ish arithmetic and list code -- which is, however, exactly
    where the hot loops are.

## Recommendation

Worth doing, and worth doing as its own multi-slice piece of work rather
than folded into another. Start maximally conservative (part 1 admitting
only builtins that cannot re-enter), get parts 2 and 3 in from the
beginning rather than promising them, and widen the analysis by
measurement.

And one point of principle, since --optimize now exists and could carry
an unsound-but-fast level the way BricsCAD's does: this engine's whole
compiler rests on compiled and interpreted evaluation agreeing, and every
slice so far has kept that by construction. A level that silently changes
what a program computes would be the first exception. If it is ever
wanted it should be its own named quality, documented as changing
semantics, and never reachable from a plain SPEED number.

## Resolution — slice 1, clautolisp 2.2.143 (2026-09-28)

pjb, 2026-09-28: "do 1 then 2." Built, sound by construction, measured.

### Two premises of the analysis turned out different in this engine

A survey of the compiler and runtime before writing any code corrected
two things above:

  - *error* does NOT run with the frame chain live here. It is a
    HANDLER-CASE (call-with-autolisp-error-handler): by the time *error*
    runs, every usubr's UNWIND-PROTECT has popped its frame, in both
    forks. So part 3 (materialise for *error*) is not needed. The only
    pre-unwind frame readers are the debugger's, under *DEBUGGING* --
    where the lexical fork never runs.
  - Escape does NOT require re-entering AutoLISP. SET, BOUNDP,
    VL-SYMBOL-VALUE, ATOMS-FAMILY and DEFUN reach a local BY NAME
    straight through the frame chain, and LOOKUP-FUNCTION walks it for a
    local in operator position.

### So the design became: never let anything look without a frame

Rather than prove that nothing escapes (a closed world plus a global
definition generation), every place where code outside the body could
run is GUARDED AT RUN TIME:

  - a call whose operator resolves -- now, at this call -- to a builtin
    AUDITED lexically safe (never calls back into AutoLISP, never touches
    a variable by name) is made directly;
  - anything else -- a user function, an unaudited builtin, a builtin
    redefined after the fork was built, SET, an interpreter fallback --
    runs with the locals MATERIALISED: pushed into a real frame with
    their current values, and copied back after it returns. Arguments
    are evaluated first, so the snapshot is current.

This replaces parts 1 and 2: the "closed world" is the per-call audit
check, and a redefinition is caught at the call instead of by a global
generation counter. Correctness therefore does not depend on the
eligibility analysis at all; the analysis only decides whether the fork
is worth BUILDING -- it is built only when no site would materialise
(a materialising site costs more than the frame the fork saves).
Refused outright, since they cannot be materialised: a local in
operator position, a FOREACH / LET rebinding a local, VLAX-FOR,
duplicate names, a local named T or NIL.

Pieces:
  - runtime: LEXICALLY-SAFE flag on subrs; LEXICAL-BODY slot on usubrs
    (the fifth body); CHECK-USUBR-ARITY split out of BIND-USUBR-FRAME so
    both refuse the same calls in the same words; a dispatch branch that
    runs the fork with no frame, never under *DEBUGGING*;
    *AUTOLISP-LEXICAL-LOCALS-ENABLED*.
  - builtins-core: *LEXICALLY-SAFE-CORE-BUILTINS* (51 builtins), stamped
    against the IMPLEMENTATION like the open-code tags; audited by the
    transitive call closure of each implementation (nothing reaches
    AUTOLISP-EVAL, CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION*, LOOKUP-/SET-VARIABLE,
    LOOKUP-FUNCTION, AUTOLISP-BOUNDP or LOAD; the only FUNCALL/APPLY met
    are host arithmetic, CONCATENATE, FORMAT, and the debug snapshot hook,
    NIL outside a session). APPLY-CLAL-OPTIMIZATION turns it on at
    (SPEED 3) (DEBUG 0) only.
  - compiler: the transpiler's lexical mode (*LEXICAL-LOCALS*), and
    COMPILE-LEXICAL-USUBR, called from COMPILE-USUBR (SPEED 3 compiles
    eagerly at DEFUN).

### Evidence

  - compiler suite: 443/443 on SBCL and on CCL, with five new tests
    (lexical-locals-tests.lisp): a 14-program corpus agreeing with the
    interpreter both normally and with forks FORCED where sites
    materialise (callee reading/writing a local, SET, BOUNDP, EVAL,
    arguments before a materialising call, recursion); where forks are
    and are not built; a builtin REDEFINED after the fork was built still
    sees the local; arity refused in the same words; never under a debug
    session.
  - mutation check: with the guard removed (calls direct, nothing
    materialised) the corpus test goes red on 10 checks and the
    redefinition test on 1 -- the tests are gates.
  - `make test-lexical-locals`: the WHOLE corpus (34 suites) with
    (SPEED 3) (DEBUG 0) and forks forced on every DEFUN: all green, 461
    lexical forks built.
  - full suite at default settings green.
  - measured (fresh processes, 3 runs each): a hot leaf function called
    10^6 times from a loop -- median 832 ms with the plain compiled fork,
    540 ms with the lexical one (1.54x on the whole loop, of which only
    the leaf got a lexical fork).

### Next slices (not done)

  - Let a lexical fork call USER functions that are themselves
    lexically safe, transitively, without materialising: the callee's
    identity must then be guarded the same way (per call), and its body
    audited -- the closed-world half, now needed only as an optimisation.
  - Allow FOREACH / LET over a local (lexical rebinding with CL LET),
    and a local in operator position (resolve through the CL variable).
  - Lexical forks in .lap artefacts (compile-file emits the plain fork
    only).
  - Widen the audited builtin list by the same transitive audit.
